# Copy to .env and adjust if needed.
# `shopify app dev` supplies SHOPIFY_API_KEY, SHOPIFY_API_SECRET, SHOPIFY_APP_URL
# and SCOPES to its own child process, so `npm run dev` doesn't need them set
# here. `npm run worker` (app/worker.ts, Phase 1 step 5) runs standalone and
# does need SHOPIFY_API_KEY and SHOPIFY_API_SECRET in .env, to build admin
# API clients for background jobs (unauthenticated.admin) and refresh expiring
# offline tokens. Run `npm run env` (shopify app env pull) once to fetch them.

# Postgres. Required: it holds the Shopify sessions (the offline access
# tokens), the product index, the saved rules and the audit log, so the app
# can't start without it. The default is the docker-compose.yml database
# (npm run db:up).
#
# It doesn't have to be local. A hosted development database (Neon, Supabase,
# Prisma Postgres) works just as well and needs no Docker: create a database,
# paste its connection string here and run `npx prisma migrate deploy` once.
# Use the direct (unpooled) connection string, not the pooled/pgbouncer one,
# so `prisma migrate` works without adding a `directUrl` to the schema. Keep
# production on a database that doesn't sleep on idle: a cold start would land
# on a merchant's request, and the product index is per shop (tens of
# thousands of rows for a large catalog).
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://catalogs:catalogs@localhost:5432/catalogs_dev?schema=public"

# Redis, for the job queue (app/lib/queue). OPTIONAL IN DEVELOPMENT: unset, the
# app serves every page and every merchant-facing action (rule builder, live
# preview, Save, Review changes, Apply to Shopify, index rebuilds, manual Sync
# now), and only skips queueing background work, with one warning per process.
# What's off while it's unset: the delayed index re-reads that catch
# collection-condition membership, and automatic syncs of managed catalogs.
# Set it (and run `npm run worker`) to test either of those. The worker itself
# requires it and fails loudly without it. Production sets it on both services.
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# The app's handle from the Partner Dashboard (the "apps/<handle>/" segment in
# its admin URL, e.g. https://admin.shopify.com/store/x/apps/<handle>/...).
# Differs between the dev and production apps. Used to build the admin links
# the Sidekick tools return (app/lib/sidekick/admin-url.ts); left unset, those
# tools omit the url field rather than guess.
SHOPIFY_APP_HANDLE="smart-catalogs-dev"
